  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Blustery and colder on Friday with rain \nshowers/elevation wintry mix.\n\nMuch of central PA will see a nondiurnal temperature trend today\nwith daily highs being reached in the predawn hours as the 995\nmb sfc low moves into NW PA and drags a nose of some warmer air\nwith it. As the system begins to pull off toward the northeast,\nits cold front will usher in cooler temps from the southwest\nthrough the day. By this afternoon, 2m temps are progged to fall\ninto the low to mid 30s in the Laurels and NW mountains.\n\nMultiple rounds of light precipitation will pivot through \ncentral Pennsylvania today as the deep upper trough associated \nwith the Thanksgiving storm moves across the area. Rain will mix\nwith or even change over to snow at times in the higher \nelevations of the Laurel Highlands and northwest mountains. \nWinds will be from the southwest much of the day, and turn more \nwesterly tonight. Wind speeds will peak in the 30-40 mph range \nover the Laurel Highlands and 20-30 mph across Southeastern PA. \nShowers will taper off on Saturday with lingering gusty winds in\nthe Laurel Highlands again gusting into the 30-40 mph range. \n\n----------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Next chance for rain arrives Sun night into Mon\n\nMedium range guidance suggests an East Coast ridge will develop\nout ahead of a digging trough coming down from the Northern\nRockies into the Great Plains on Sunday. This should promote a\nbrief window of clearing skies early Saturday night, but then\nstratus becomes possible with an approaching warm front in the\nwest, and patchy valley fog will be possible to the east of the\narea with stratus. Sunday looks to be a warmer day compared to \nSaturday, but clouds will continue to increase through the day \nahead of the approaching trough. By early Sunday evening, rain \nwill likely be moving into the western highlands, then spreading\neastward through Sunday night. Model consensus takes the sfc \nlow through the eastern Great Lakes with a sweeping cold front \nmoving through the area on Monday, and potential for lake effect\nrain/snow showers later in the day Monday. Most likely rainfall\nis in the 0.25-1.00 inch range. Approx 50 pct of the membership\nof the NBM suggests wind advisory criteria (45+ mph gusts) will\nbe met in the Laurel Highlands with westerly wind gusts along \nand behind the cold front on Monday.",
